From 3561b9d9383f90d95ae85202c33a215b450ca437 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: spotUP <retroranksmail@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:22:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 47/47] upkg: stable exit codes and one machine-readable
 progress line per step

Front-ends (Amarchy, scripts) need to tell a missing package from a dead
network from a failing package script without parsing messages.

Exit codes sit on the AmigaDOS bands: 0 ok, 5 WARN (nothing matched or
nothing to remove), 10 ERROR (reserved), 11 usage (also browse without a
terminal), 12 package not found, 13 network, 14 package script failed
(preinst, postinst, prerm, postrm, or a uhc: script returning nonzero; the
script's own number goes to stderr), 15 interactive package without a
terminal, 20 FAIL (start-up failure, unclassified). The first failure noted
with upkg_note_fail() decides the class of a negative internal result;
upkg_rc() maps it. update with failed feeds now returns that class instead
of the failure count. install --all asks up front, so it is exempt from the
rc 15 check (upkg_all_seeded()).

Progress: with stdout not a terminal, or UPKG_PROGRESS=1, each step prints
"[PROGRESS n/m] <phase> <name>" through opkg_msg(NOTICE): update, install,
download, unpack, configure, remove, and script for uhc: packages. No
dependence on terminal width; tty output is unchanged. upkg --help points
at doc/upkg-api.txt.
